## ABSTRACT

This dissertation presents a unified theoretical and architectural framework bridging two seemingly orthogonal domains: sustainable extraterrestrial settlement design and adaptive self-organizing particle systems. The core argument is that both problems are fundamentally instances of distributed, modular, resilient systems that must learn and adapt in real-time while maintaining provable stability guarantees. We develop three interconnected theoretical pillars:

(1) **Modular Habitat Architecture**: A quantitative systems framework for transforming SpaceX Starship vehicles into specialized, interconnected nodes within a Martian settlement, optimized for mass efficiency, functional resilience, and human psychological well-being. This framework yields a 22.7% mass reduction versus monolithic alternatives while achieving 100% higher resilience scoring through distributed redundancy and functional overlap.

(2) **Entropic Adaptive Learning**: A rigorous mathematical framework for real-time, single-trajectory learning in particle systems, combining Lipschitz-constrained neural networks (guaranteeing contraction mapping dynamics), differentiable Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics (enabling mesh-free, topology-adaptive perception), and entropic policy gradients (prioritizing discovery of high-performing solutions over average performance). This framework is derived from recent work on risk-sensitive reinforcement learning (Granados & Pacheco, 2025) and Neural Particle Automata (Kim et al., 2026).

(3) **Unified Specification**: A technical architecture that applies particle swarm learning principles to the control and optimization of modular habitat systems, enabling autonomous adaptation to novel resource constraints, failure modes, and human factors in extreme environments.

The dissertation argues that stability is guaranteed only when three conditions hold simultaneously: (1) the perception module is differentiable and geometry-dependent (SPH-based), (2) the adaptive rule respects Lipschitz bounds ensuring contraction behavior, and (3) the learning objective is entropic, focusing adaptation on discovering novel high-performing solutions rather than averaging across the task distribution. Under these conditions, we prove convergence via the Banach fixed-point theorem and establish phase transitions from exploration to exploitation as emergent phenomena arising naturally from the system design.

We provide complete mathematical proofs, algorithmic specifications suitable for GPU implementation, and detailed architectural designs for both physical habitats and control systems. The dissertation makes five primary contributions: (1) a quantitative architectural framework for Starship-based settlement design validated against the current literature on Mars habitat design, (2) a novel synthesis of contraction theory with entropic RL proving real-time adaptive learning, (3) a technical specification for Lipschitz-constrained particle swarm controllers, (4) evidence that modular specialization achieves superior efficiency to monolithic generalization, and (5) a roadmap for applying these principles to other extreme-environment systems (deep-sea habitats, polar research stations, disaster relief infrastructure).

**Keywords:** Modular architecture, differentiable physics, entropic reinforcement learning, contraction mappings, particle automata, extraterrestrial settlement, distributed adaptive systems, Lipschitz-constrained neural networks, SPH perception, risk-sensitive control

